### What is eager initialization?

```java
public class ConnectionPool {
    private static final ConnectionPool INSTANCE = new ConnectionPool();
    private ConnectionPool() {}
    public static ConnectionPool getInstance() { return INSTANCE; }
}
```

Thread-safe because the JVM guarantees class initialization is thread-safe. Downside: instance is created even if never used.

### What is synchronized getInstance?

```java
public class ConnectionPool {
    private static ConnectionPool instance;
    private ConnectionPool() {}
    public static synchronized ConnectionPool getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) instance = new ConnectionPool();
        return instance;
    }
}
```

Lazy and correct but every call pays the lock cost.

### What is double-checked locking?

```java
public class ConnectionPool {
    private static volatile ConnectionPool instance;  // MUST be volatile
    private ConnectionPool() {}
    public static ConnectionPool getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {                       // first check (no lock)
            synchronized (ConnectionPool.class) {
                if (instance == null) {               // second check (locked)
                    instance = new ConnectionPool();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

The `volatile` keyword is mandatory. Without it, another thread could see a non-null reference to an object whose constructor has not finished.

### What is the Bill Pugh holder idiom?

```java
public class ConnectionPool {
    private ConnectionPool() {}
    private static class Holder {
        static final ConnectionPool INSTANCE = new ConnectionPool();
    }
    public static ConnectionPool getInstance() { return Holder.INSTANCE; }
}
```

The inner class is not loaded until `getInstance` references it, so initialization is lazy. The JVM's class-init lock makes it thread-safe.

### What is the enum singleton?

```java
public enum ConnectionPool {
    INSTANCE;
    // methods and state here
}
```

Simplest. The JVM guarantees a single instance, and it is immune to serialization and reflection attacks.

### How do they compare?

| Approach | Lazy | Thread-safe | Serialization-safe | Reflection-safe |
|----------|------|-------------|-------------------|-----------------|
| Eager | No | Yes | Needs readResolve | No |
| Synchronized | Yes | Yes | Needs readResolve | No |
| Double-checked | Yes | Yes (if volatile) | Needs readResolve | No |
| Bill Pugh | Yes | Yes | Needs readResolve | No |
| Enum | No | Yes | Yes | Yes |

